title: File similarity
tags: [[Reconcile]]
kind: reference
summary: With --threshold, reconcile scores unmatched orphans against unmatched new paths on name, extension, size and directory — for files both moved and edited.

The similarity phase is a fallback for what the fingerprint cannot match: an orphan with
no stored partial hash, or a file both moved ''and'' modified. It runs only when a
reconcile is given a threshold (`--threshold <0..1>`, `{"threshold": …}`), on the
whole repository.

It compares the orphans and the new paths ''left unmatched'' by the fingerprint phase —
usually few — file with file and directory with directory, and scores each pair:

|!Signal |!Measures |!Weight |
|name |trigram Jaccard similarity of the base names, extension removed |0.5 |
|extension |1 when identical, else 0 |0.2 |
|size |`max(0, 1 − |a − b| / max(a, b))`, 0 when a size is unknown |0.2 |
|directory |common directory prefix length over the greater depth |0.1 |

Pairs scoring at least the threshold are offered as candidates with `"fingerprint":
"similarity"` and their `score`; nothing is decided for you
([[Moves and candidates]]).

''Proposed, not built'': for repositories whose ''unmatched'' sets are themselves huge,
MinHash banding over name trigrams would find candidate pairs in expected linear time
instead of scoring every pair, after a size pre-filter; and a locality-sensitive content
hash (TLSH) could add a content signal for slightly modified files. Neither library is a
dependency today.
